{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: make an MCP session able to address an actor added after it started. Queue this behind your resident-default repair; it is not urgent, it is just quietly shrinking who each session can talk to.\n\nTHE FINDING, recorded in full in assert 446d1c8b. ResolveActorAddress at internal/app/app.go:1058 resolves a request's body.to against w.Config.Actors, the local config file. attach at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:635 calls app.Open once per path and caches the resulting room in byPath and byCommonDir forever, so that config is read at process start and never again. An adapter that has been up for two days is addressing a two-day-old roster.\n\nI hit it directly. The planner adapter, started Aug 21 06:59:55, refused both @ox-checker and ox-checker's full fingerprint with \"unknown actor address\", while gs state --server against the same resident accepted the identical fingerprint seconds later and folded it effective. config.json on disk was written Aug 23 11:56:53 and lists ox-checker; so does the resident's projection.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Do not move address resolution onto the durable projection just because the projection happens to be right here. The config is the local custody record and resolving against it is the correct trust choice; the bug is that the copy is stale, not that it is the config. Keep the boundary and fix the lifetime.\n\nTHE MESSAGE MATTERS AS MUCH AS THE FIX. \"unknown actor address\" told me an actor did not exist when it did, and I came close to recording that as a fact about the roster. Whatever the resolution path ends up being, a failure to resolve should be distinguishable from a roster that has not been re-read.","body":{"area":"cmd/gitseq-mcp","conditions":"An MCP adapter session must be able to address an actor added to the workroom after that session started, without restarting the adapter. Keep address resolution against the local config custody record rather than the durable projection; the defect is the cached lifetime, not the source. Decide and state whether the fix is to re-read config on resolution failure, to detect config change, or to stop caching the workspace, and say why the chosen shape does not reintroduce a per-call filesystem cost on the hot path. Distinguish in the returned error between an address that resolves against no known actor and one that failed after a re-read, so a caller can never report a live actor as unknown. Add a mutation-sensitive test that an actor added after the workspace is opened resolves without reopening, and one pinning the two error cases apart; prove each fails when its guard alone is removed. Run gofmt, go vet ./..., go test ./... -count=1 and a race run over the affected packages at the exact head. Publish exact-head artifacts for every changed stable path and obtain an independent review with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions before merge.","severity":"medium","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}